Samsung's first AI glasses are called Intelligent Eyewear, and they reach pre-order stage today according to Warby Parker's own timeline. This guide separates what the companies have confirmed from what has only been reported, because the gap is large: there is still no published price.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages. Stackbrook did not test the glasses, and has no affiliate relationship with Samsung, Google, Warby Parker, Gentle Monster or Meta at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

Labels used below: "confirmed" means a company's own page or release says it. "Reported" means a news outlet says it and the company has not published it in the sources we checked.

## What Samsung has confirmed

Samsung introduced the eyewear at Galaxy Unpacked in London and describes them as built in partnership with Google, bringing Gemini into a familiar wearable form. Its newsroom post, dated 22 July 2026, confirms these specifics:

- **A camera.** Samsung says the built-in camera gives the assistant a view of what you see, and that the eyewear can capture it when prompted. No resolution or sensor details are given.
- **Gemini, through a phone.** Samsung's footnote says the glasses need a Samsung and Google sign-in and a paired Galaxy AI phone with a data connection.
- **Battery.** Samsung rates battery at up to nine hours per charge, with the case adding up to seven more full charges. It notes that this varies with use.
- **Chip.** The Snapdragon AR1 Gen1 platform.
- **Design partners.** Samsung says Gentle Monster and Warby Parker developed the frames. Press coverage says the Gemini integration runs on Google's Android XR.

Samsung's earlier 20 May announcement at Google I/O 2026 said the first collections would launch "this fall in select markets," and that specifications are subject to change. Warby Parker's 19 May release said its first line would include multiple optical and sun styles.

## What is only reported

Several details people are repeating have not appeared in a Samsung, Google or Warby Parker release that we could find.

- **No display (reported).** 9to5Google describes the glasses as audio-only with no in-lens display, controlled by voice and a side touchpad. Samsung's newsroom posts do not mention a display either way.
- **Weight of about 46 g (reported).** A Let's Data Science summary of local Korean media says local media report Samsung is targeting up to 5 g below the 51 g it attributes to the base Ray-Ban Meta, which works out to about 46 g. Samsung has not released a final weight, and its post says only "lightweight frame and slim profile".
- **Works with iPhone (reported, contested).** VR.org says Google is reported to state that the glasses pair with both Android and iOS phones. Samsung's own footnote, described above, names a compatible Galaxy AI phone. Until that is settled, assume a Galaxy phone is the safe requirement.
- **Markets and timing.** VR.org says a 30 October ChosunBiz report put sales in South Korea and the US in mid-December, which Samsung has not confirmed. Samsung reportedly told The Korea Herald only "November".

## Status: announced, pre-order or on sale?

The glasses are not on sale yet. Warby Parker's third-quarter earnings statement, as quoted by 9to5Google and VR.org, says it will begin accepting pre-orders for its first Intelligent Eyewear collection on 12 November 2026, and expects to deliver orders "during the holiday season." We read the date through those two reports rather than the filing itself. VR.org adds that Samsung and Google had not confirmed the date when it published.

On price, both reports say no company has announced one, and VR.org treats circulating figures as unreliable leaks. Treat any number you see as unconfirmed. The same goes for an exact ship date: Warby Parker says "holiday season", Gizmodo reports its co-CEO saying December, and Gentle Monster's page reportedly says December, but no exact ship date has been published. If the Warby Parker pre-order page shows a price today, that page is the source to trust over this article.

## How it compares with Ray-Ban Meta

Ray-Ban Meta is the product Samsung is chasing. Meta's pages show the Gen 2 glasses as available now from $379, with Meta AI and 3K video capture. Meta's Gen 2 pages list camera, Meta AI, open-ear audio and calling, and show no display.

![Status table comparing Samsung Intelligent Eyewear and Ray-Ban Meta on display, camera, assistant, status and price, with confirmed or reported labels](/images/site1/samsung-intelligent-eyewear-ai-glasses-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the company pages and press reports cited in this article.*

The practical differences, on what is known so far:

- **Assistant.** Samsung's glasses use Gemini, which suits people already in Google and Samsung accounts. Ray-Ban Meta uses Meta AI.
- **Availability.** Ray-Ban Meta can be bought now. Samsung's can only be pre-ordered, with the price missing.
- **Frames.** Samsung's come from Gentle Monster and Warby Parker, which matters if you need prescription lenses. Warby Parker's 19 May release says the line will support a broad range of prescriptions and lens options, but gives no pricing or lens costs.
- **Phone dependence.** Samsung's footnote ties the glasses to a connected phone. Check Meta's own requirements before assuming the same for Ray-Ban Meta.

## The market context

A report from Counterpoint Research, as covered by 9to5Google and MIXED, gives the backdrop. Global AI glasses shipments rose 263% year on year in the first half of 2026, and 96% of them had no display. MIXED says Meta accounted for about 94% of the display-less segment, while 9to5Google words it as Meta holding 94% of the whole market. The two outlets frame the figure differently, so quote it with care. 9to5Google notes that no unit figures were shared, so these are growth rates and shares, not volumes. The point for buyers is that Meta has a large lead, and Samsung is entering a segment Meta dominates.

## Privacy and camera etiquette

A camera on your face affects other people, so check this before buying. Samsung's newsroom post mentions only "clear controls and safeguards" for responsible use and does not describe a recording indicator. Android Authority is reported, via Let's Data Science, as saying there are indicators inside and outside the frame and that the camera shuts off if the outside one is covered. We could not confirm that from Samsung, so treat it as unverified. Samsung also notes that features such as translation, navigation and live visual sharing may vary by region and local law. In practice, ask before recording in private spaces, and read the final product's privacy documentation once it exists.

## Before you pre-order

Wait for the Warby Parker page to show the price, the lens options, the refund terms and the ship date, since none are confirmed elsewhere. Check that your phone is supported. If you want a camera-and-assistant pair of glasses today, Ray-Ban Meta is the only one of the two you can actually buy.
